The important drafting principle is not merely to provide a huge list of alternatives. We need enough structure and examples to demonstrate possession of the combinations we may later claim. USPTO's written-description guidance states that later claim limitations must be expressly, implicitly, or inherently supported in the originally filed disclosure. (MPEP)

Supplemental provisional strategy

There is also a useful mechanism for developments during the 12-month period.

If we conceive a materially new implementation after Provisional A is filed, we should not attempt to amend Provisional A. Instead, we can consider filing Provisional B containing the new material.

A later nonprovisional may claim the benefit of one or more prior-filed provisional applications under 37 C.F.R. §1.78, provided the applicable requirements are satisfied. (USPTO)

Conceptually:

Provisional A - Day 0
    broad architecture
    recognition
    lethal control
    population logic

Provisional B - Day 80
    improved individual-ID algorithm
    additional safety architecture

Provisional C - Day 190
    improved population-completeness mechanism

                    ↓

Nonprovisional - before applicable 12-month deadline
    claims benefit of A + B + C

The priority analysis is then based on what each earlier filing actually supports. A later claim does not automatically receive Day 0 merely because A is somewhere in the priority chain; the claimed subject matter must be adequately supported and enabled by A to obtain A's date. (USPTO)

That gives us a useful rolling disclosure-capture strategy during the provisional year.

Tracker changes

I added a Decision Log recording:

DEC-001 - Provisional claim strategy

The 20-claim architecture is an internal claim-development/search/restriction tool only. It does not limit the provisional specification and is not a filing gate.

DEC-002 - Later-developed subject matter

Material post-filing improvements are to be evaluated for additional provisional filings rather than attempted amendments to the original provisional.

I also added:

PAT-015 - Broad disclosure-support audit

PAT-016 - Supplemental-provisional decision process

and revised the existing filing tasks so that §112(a) disclosure quality-not claim completion-is the filing gate.

Practical drafting consequence

I would now reorganize our workflow slightly:

Specification first, claims as probes.

We can still draft Claims 1, mid-scope, and 20 because they are useful tools for asking:

"If we eventually wanted this claim, where is every limitation supported?"

But when those claims expose an unsupported embodiment, the answer should usually be to expand the specification, not prematurely narrow the claim.

That approach is much better aligned with the purpose of this provisional.

Next steps for next run

Build the provisional specification around a broad disclosure matrix, not the 20-claim set.

Create a claim-to-spec support matrix for contemplated broad, intermediate, and narrow claim concepts.

Enumerate alternative pest classes, recognition modalities, decision logic, targeting/delivery mechanisms, and lethal mechanisms.

Add concrete embodiments and technical examples supporting important combinations rather than relying on generic laundry-list language.

Establish a dated post-filing invention log for improvements conceived during the 12-month provisional period.

Evaluate additional provisional filings whenever materially new subject matter arises.

Continue using Set 1 claims as patentability/support probes, while removing claim completion from the provisional filing critical path.

